WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:07.910
What do you see when you go to the drugstore? Shelves full of pills to help with all 

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:08.960
sorts of ailments,

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:09.800
from headaches,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.740
to allergies.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.340
What we don't see are the years of research that led to that little bottle on the 

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:20.820
shelf or the hundreds of drugs that were tried and tested along the way.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.160
Let's walk through the very long and expensive, but a thorough journey of getting a new 

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.490
medicine to the shelf,

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.360
shining a spotlight on the challenges faced at every stage.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.020
But first,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.240
what is a drug? For this discussion,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.820
we're talking about small organic molecules. Two great examples of these are 

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.150
aspirin and caffeine.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:46.530
Drugs such as these work by binding to what is called a target. The drug 

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.420
tweaks the activity of the target,

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.750
making it more or less active.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.250
So how do scientists find drugs?

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:54.250
Some drugs,

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.160
such as caffeine,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.110
come from nature,

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:57.000
but others,

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:57.990
like aspirin,

00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.580
are made by chemists.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.840
But it can be difficult to figure out which molecular shapes are most likely to 

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.150
be successful drugs.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.550
So what to do?

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:08.290
Well,

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:12.970
a common method is to make hundreds of thousands of different 

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:18.030
compounds and then sort them to find ones that bind specifically to the target and 

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.200
can tweak its activity.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.250
This is called high throughput screening.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:23.340
If you're lucky,

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.120
you get a few hit compounds.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.750
These hits usually need to be further chemically optimized,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.520
which is a tricky process.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.300
However,

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.940
compounds that do make it through are tested for safety and efficacy in 

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.210
animals such as mice.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:43.220
Many drugs that work in a test tube or Petri dish don't work when given to an 

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.990
animal or have adverse effects. If the drug makes it through this 

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:48.790
filter step,

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.200
which is another valley of death for drugs,

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:56.360
researchers investigate the best way to deliver the medicine to people, in a pill, with 

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.630
a patch, in an injection.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.250
How does the body absorb,

00:02:00.255 --> 00:02:01.075
distribute,

00:02:01.085 --> 00:02:03.995
breakdown and excrete the drug?

00:02:04.395 --> 00:02:05.355
By this point,

00:02:05.365 --> 00:02:10.325
many years have gone by. After establishing efficacy and safety in animals,

00:02:10.555 --> 00:02:15.225
an investigational new drug, IND, application can now be filed with the 

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:15.885
FDA,

00:02:15.955 --> 00:02:17.765
the Food and Drug Administration.

00:02:18.495 --> 00:02:20.005
If the FDA approves,

00:02:20.135 --> 00:02:22.805
the drug can move into human clinical trials.

00:02:23.335 --> 00:02:28.195
The first of these are phase I clinical trials to establish any adverse effects 

00:02:28.205 --> 00:02:29.595
of the drug in humans.

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:32.225
If safety is established,

00:02:32.235 --> 00:02:36.965
a phase II clinical trial is next, to see if the drug is effective 

00:02:37.165 --> 00:02:40.765
in treating the disease in a relatively small number of patients.

00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:43.895
There are many important decisions to make.

00:02:44.235 --> 00:02:48.695
Who should get the medicine? For how long? Does it work better than current treatments?

00:02:48.905 --> 00:02:51.045
How will we know if it really works?

00:02:51.095 --> 00:02:54.565
These trials are usually performed in a blind fashion,

00:02:54.895 --> 00:02:59.525
which means neither the patient nor the physician know whether an experimental 

00:02:59.525 --> 00:03:04.145
drug or control, meaning no drug or a known drug, is being 

00:03:04.145 --> 00:03:04.725
administered.

00:03:05.415 --> 00:03:08.705
If the phase II trial data shows patient safety and efficacy,

00:03:09.135 --> 00:03:11.505
then a phase III trial is conducted.

00:03:12.155 --> 00:03:15.095
These trials require a larger number of patients,

00:03:15.405 --> 00:03:18.115
cost millions of dollars and can take years.

00:03:18.595 --> 00:03:19.305
However,

00:03:19.485 --> 00:03:20.985
if the data is positive,

00:03:21.135 --> 00:03:25.145
a new drug application or NDA is submitted to the FDA,

00:03:25.155 --> 00:03:26.345
and if approved,

00:03:26.545 --> 00:03:29.275
the drug can finally be marketed to treat patients.

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:32.795
Sometimes for special cases such as epidemics,

00:03:33.035 --> 00:03:35.975
life threatening diseases or unmet medical needs,

00:03:36.155 --> 00:03:38.085
medicines can be fast tracked,

00:03:38.355 --> 00:03:42.895
an option developed by the FDA in 1988 at the height of the AIDS 

00:03:42.895 --> 00:03:43.535
epidemic.

00:03:44.275 --> 00:03:45.045
Naturally,

00:03:45.055 --> 00:03:46.865
even after a drug is on market,

00:03:47.205 --> 00:03:50.595
drug safety and efficacy continue to be monitored.

00:03:50.975 --> 00:03:55.715
Sometimes, rare side effects only show up when many people take the medicine.

00:03:56.755 --> 00:03:57.125
Whew!

00:03:57.575 --> 00:03:59.365
That was a lot of work to make a drug.

00:03:59.375 --> 00:04:04.002
But the truth is, that it's even more involved. Prior to starting drug 

00:04:04.002 --> 00:04:07.122
discovery, researchers often in universities,

00:04:07.262 --> 00:04:12.232
spend years or decades trying to understand a disease and to find potential 

00:04:12.232 --> 00:04:13.392
relevant drug targets.

00:04:13.402 --> 00:04:16.002
We still don't know enough about many diseases,

00:04:16.292 --> 00:04:20.152
which is why we don't have good drugs for Alzheimer's or Huntington's disease.

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:24.062
Although the road to a new drug is a lot of hard work,

00:04:24.072 --> 00:04:25.012
time, and money,

00:04:25.352 --> 00:04:30.022
I think you'd agree that the drug discovery process gives us and our loved ones an 

00:04:30.032 --> 00:04:32.742
opportunity to have healthy and happy lives.

00:04:33.182 --> 00:04:38.082
And it gives us a sense of security that much testing has been done when we pick up 

00:04:38.082 --> 00:04:39.652
those pills from the pharmacy. 

